From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 23:36:49 +0200
Subject: riscv: dts: sophgo: put the Milk-V Duo S USB port in host mode

The Duo S shares one dwc2 controller between the Type-C receptacle and the
Type-A port. Mainline brings it up with dr_mode = "otg" and no way to choose,
and the board-level plumbing is missing too: phy-cv1800-usb2.c owns the ID
override and VBUS_POWER bits in the PHY's syscon register, but nothing drives
the two GPIOs that gate power to the Type-A port, so a peripheral plugged in
there never comes up.

The known userspace answer to this (queenkjuul's milkv-usb-duos) writes the
syscon register through /dev/mem and drives the two lines with libgpiod. That
races the PHY driver's regmap and clobbers VBUS_POWER, which the write of a
whole 32-bit word cannot preserve. Both halves have a device tree equivalent.

For the role: dr_mode = "host". The obvious alternative - keep otg, add
usb-role-switch and role-switch-default-mode = "host", and retain the ability
to select device mode at runtime - hangs the boot on this SoC. dwc2_drd_init()
calls dwc2_ovr_init(), which forces the session-valid overrides in GOTGCTL and
then dwc2_force_mode(); dwc2_hcd_init() still completes, and the
usb_add_gadget_udc() after it never returns. Because this is a deferred probe,
deferred_probe_initcall() never completes either, so the machine stops before
clk_disable_unused() with no panic and no further output:

  hub 1-0:1.0: 1 port detected
  probe of 1-0:1.0 returned 0 after 8613 usecs
  probe of usb1 returned 0 after 14736 usecs
  <nothing, ever>

initcall_debug is what makes that legible - "probe of 4340000.usb returned 0"
never appears. dr_mode = "host" avoids the whole mechanism: dwc2 leaves
gadget_enabled clear, so neither the gadget nor the role switch is set up. The
cost is the Type-C receptacle's device mode, which this board can afford - it
has ethernet, so headless setup does not need USB networking.

For the power: the GPIOs become a fixed regulator each, reached by phy-supply
on the PHY. USB_VBUS_EN is XGPIOB_5 and AUX0 is XGPIOA_30 in mux function 3,
per Milk-V's own pinmux table; AUX0's role on the board is undocumented, but it
is absent from the Duo 256M variant of the same script, so it is Duo S
specific. They are chained through vin-supply to keep them in the order the
script uses.

The pads are muxed from the regulator nodes rather than from &usb, so the mux
is applied before the regulators drive the lines: the pin controller is strict,
implements no gpio_request_enable, and the GPIO banks declare no gpio-ranges,
so requesting a line does not mux its pad. Both groups carry power-source,
which is not optional - cv1800_dt_node_to_map_post() fails the whole map
without it, and the consumer then never probes at all.

VBUS follows phy_power_on() rather than the role, which is now moot in a
host-only configuration.

Tested on a Duo S: the controller probes, the root hub enumerates, and the
board reaches a login prompt.

Signed-off-by: Armbian <info@armbian.com>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts | 79 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 78 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-milkv-duo-s.dts
@@ -32,6 +32,39 @@ chosen {
 		stdout-path = "serial0:115200n8";
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * Type-A port power. Two board lines have to be driven for the port to
+	 * come up, and neither is wired to anything the USB controller drives by
+	 * itself: USB_VBUS_EN, which per Milk-V's own pinmux table is XGPIOB_5
+	 * when muxed to function 3, and AUX0, which is XGPIOA_30. They are
+	 * chained so the regulator core brings AUX0 up first and takes it down
+	 * last, which is the order the vendor's userspace switcher uses.
+	 *
+	 * AUX0's exact role on this board is undocumented - the Duo 256M switcher
+	 * needs no GPIO at all, so it is specific to the Duo S - hence the plain
+	 * name rather than a guess at what it gates.
+	 */
+	reg_usb_aux0: regulator-usb-aux0 {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb-aux0";
+		gpio = <&porta 30 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&usb_aux0_cfg>;
+	};
+
+	reg_usb_vbus: regulator-usb-vbus {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb-vbus";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		gpio = <&portb 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+		vin-supply = <&reg_usb_aux0>;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&usb_vbus_en_cfg>;
+	};
+
 	sound_card {
 		compatible = "simple-audio-card";
 		simple-audio-card,name = "Milk-V Duo S Built-in Audio";
@@ -190,11 +223,55 @@ &uart4 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * The pads are muxed from the regulator nodes rather than from &usb, so the
+ * mux is in place before the regulators drive the lines. The pin controller is
+ * strict and has no gpio_request_enable, and the GPIO banks declare no
+ * gpio-ranges, so requesting the line does not mux the pad by itself.
+ */
+&pinctrl {
+	usb_aux0_cfg: usb-aux0-cfg {
+		usb-aux0-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_AUX0, 3)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	usb_vbus_en_cfg: usb-vbus-en-cfg {
+		usb-vbus-en-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_USB_VBUS_EN, 3)>;
+			power-source = <1800>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * Host only, not otg with a role switch. dwc2 hangs in probe on this SoC when
+ * usb-role-switch is set: dwc2_drd_init() runs dwc2_ovr_init(), which forces
+ * the session-valid overrides in GOTGCTL and calls dwc2_force_mode(), and the
+ * usb_add_gadget_udc() that follows dwc2_hcd_init() never returns. The probe
+ * is a deferred one, so deferred_probe_initcall() never completes either, and
+ * the boot stops dead before clk_disable_unused() - no output, no panic, the
+ * last line is "hub 1-0:1.0: 1 port detected".
+ *
+ * dr_mode = "host" reaches the same default by a shorter route: dwc2 leaves
+ * gadget_enabled clear, so neither the gadget nor the role switch is set up at
+ * all. The Type-C receptacle gives up its device mode for good, which is the
+ * trade this board wants - it has ethernet for headless work.
+ */
 &usb {
-	dr_mode = "otg";
+	dr_mode = "host";
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * phy-supply is enabled by phy_power_on(), which dwc2 calls when it brings the
+ * controller up.
+ */
+&usbphy {
+	phy-supply = <&reg_usb_vbus>;
+};
+
 &wdt {
 	status = "okay";
 };
-- 
Armbian

